The year 2021 marked the 100th anniversary of the Mongolian People's Party and the [[Mongolian Revolution of 1921|1921 revolution]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ankhtuyaa|first=B. |date=2021-03-01 |title=Mongolian People's Party turns 100 - News.MN |url=https://news.mn/en/795400/ |access-date=2025-08-27 |website=News.MN |language=en}}</ref> In April 29, just six days before the nomination deadline, the MPRP agreed to merge back into the MPP after nearly a decade of separation.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 April 2021 |title=МАН, МАХН нэгдэн нийлэх гэрээнд гарын үсэг зурлаа |trans-title=MPP and MPRP sign agreement to merge |url=https://montsame.mn/mn/read/262239 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210429112021/https://montsame.mn/mn/read/262239 |archive-date=29 April 2021 |access-date=5 October 2021 |website=Montsame News Agency |language=mn}}</ref> This strategic merger narrowed the amount of candidates in the 2021 race from 4 to 3. The MPRP had been the third-largest party in Mongolia and in the [[State Great Khural]] since 2012. Former president and MPRP chairman [[Nambaryn Enkhbayar]] backed Khurelsukh for the presidency.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mongolian People's Party, Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party merge |url=https://montsame.mn/en/read/262327 |access-date=2025-09-05 |website=Montsame News Agency |language=en}}</ref>

=== Democratic Party ===
Political infightings within the DP reached a boiling point in February when presidential nominee hopeful, [[Oyungerel Tsedevdamba|Tsedevdambyn Oyungerel]] backed Erdene against interim leader Tuvaan's faction loyal to Battulga. Tuvaan and his allies exiled Erdene from the party for 8 years and in return Erdene excused Tuvaan from his interim position in an online post. In the subsequent following days, many veteran DP politicians and MPs sided against Battulga's renomination but not on the side of Erdene.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ariunzaya|first=B.|date=2021-02-01 |title=Ц.Туваан С.Эрдэнийг "хөөж", С.Эрдэнэ Ц.Тувааныг чөлөөлөв |url=https://tovch.mn/n/162c?utm_source=Mongolia+Weekly&utm_campaign=223c99352e-EMAIL_CAMPAIGN_2019_11_28_11_33_COPY_01&utm_medium=email&utm_term=0_9a59177f81-223c99352e-28259541&ct=t(Y_COPY_01)&mc_cid=223c99352e&mc_eid=4e4dfc7be4 |access-date=2025-09-06 |website=Tovch |language=mn}}</ref>

Campaign for the next party chairman kicked off in March between 9 candidates. Approximately 200,000 Democratic Party members and supporters were eligible to vote online for the next leader.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-03-19 |title=Ардчилсан намын даргад нэр дэвшигчдийн цахим уулзалт амжилттай үргэлжилж байна |url=https://ikon.mn/n/25ux |access-date=2025-09-05 |website=Ikon|language=mn}}</ref> Two primaries were held by two sides of the party, which led to the party having two elected party chairman, Mainbayaryn Tulgat ally of Erdene and [[Odongiin Tsogtgerel]] ally of Tuvaan.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-03-27 |title=Ардчилсан намын даргын сонгуульд нэр дэвшигчдийн олон нийтийн санал асуулгыг Одонгийн Цогтгэрэл тэргүүлжээ |url=https://www.zindaa.mn/3ndn |access-date=2025-09-05 |website=Zindaa|language=mn}}</ref> The [[Supreme Court of Mongolia]] refused to register neither as the party chairman, stating that "this is an internal party matter that the Supreme Court should not be involved with".<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2021-04-28 |title=АН нэгдэж чадалгүй ЕРӨНХИЙЛӨГЧИЙН СОНГУУЛИЙГ өнжих үү! |url=https://www.zindaa.mn/3oz4 |access-date=2025-09-09 |website=Zindaa|language=mn}}</ref> The DP was unable to unify under a single chairman before the June elections and Battulga increasingly distanced himself from the political infighting.
